@startuml
title Command Execution Pipeline - Redis-like Server

' One connection's rounds: read | parse | execute | write, repeated.
' Reading, parsing and writing run on the session's I/O thread; executing
' runs on the keyspace thread with --io-threads above 1, or in line.

start
:Accept the connection
(refused with "-ERR max number of clients reached"
past --maxclients);
repeat
  :Wait for bytes
  (closed after --idle-timeout, if set);
  :Read them into the session's buffer;
  if (Buffer past --client-query-buffer-limit?) then (yes)
    :Close the connection;
    stop
  endif
  :parse_pending(): cut every complete request out of the buffer
  (RESP arrays, or inline commands from telnet and redis-benchmark);
  note right
    A request split across reads waits for the rest of it;
    one read can carry several pipelined requests,
    which then run as one batch.
  end note
  :Execute the batch
  (closed after --request-timeout, if set);
  repeat
    :Look up the command name, in any case;
    if (Known command?) then (yes)
      if (Arity matches?) then (yes)
        :Run it on the keyspace
        (typed lookups: WRONGTYPE for a key of another type);
      else (no)
        :Reply "ERR wrong number of arguments for '<cmd>' command";
      endif
    else (no)
      :Reply "ERR unknown command '<name>', with args beginning with: ...";
    endif
  repeat while (More requests in the batch,
  and replies under --client-output-buffer-limit?) is (yes)
  :Append the batch's writes to the AOF, if it is on
  (and fsync first with appendfsync always);
  :Write every reply of the batch in one write;
repeat while (Protocol error, or output past the limit?) is (no)
-> yes;
:Reply "-ERR Protocol error: ..." if that was the cause;
:Close the connection;
stop
@enduml
